<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Pure-managed zstd port, no P/Invoke. DBN uses Zstandard for transport compression. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="ZstdSharp.Port" Version="0.8.8" />
    <!--
      All date and time handling, per CLAUDE.md. NodaTime's Instant carries true nanosecond
      precision; a BCL DateTime tick is 100 ns and cannot represent a DBN timestamp at all.
      Zero dependencies of its own, and clean under this repo's trim and AOT analyzers.

      This is the second package that reaches consumers transitively, because NodaTime types
      appear in the public API (MappingInterval, the symbol maps, DbnTime). That cost was a
      deliberate call in #17, not an accident.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="NodaTime" Version="3.3.3" />
    <!--
      This is the third package that reaches consumers transitively, because
      HistoricalClient.LoggerFactory is a public property (D2, #35). Pure abstractions — no
      provider, no console writer — so it brings nothing to wire up on its own; it resolves to
      NullLogger.Instance and logs nothing when a caller configures no factory. AOT-clean.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Build">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" Version="8.0.0" />
    <!--
      Turns the NodaTime convention into a build failure rather than a habit. RS0030 fires on
      every banned symbol in BannedSymbols.txt, and TreatWarningsAsErrors promotes it to an
      error naming the NodaTime replacement.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers" Version="4.14.0" />
    <!--
      Turns the public API surface into a reviewed file. RS0016 fires on a public member with no
      entry in PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt and RS0017 on an entry with no member, both promoted to
      errors by TreatWarningsAsErrors — so adding, removing or retyping anything public becomes a
      diff somebody reads rather than something noticed after 1.0.

      5.6.0 rather than 4.14.0 to match the line above, and the mismatch is deliberate. This
      codebase leans on `required` init properties, `readonly record struct` and static abstract
      interface members, all of which the 4.x formatter renders differently or not at all; the
      baseline this analyzer writes is only as good as its understanding of the surface. Both
      packages ship 5.6.0 and BannedApiAnalyzers is a major version behind — that bump is its own
      chore, not this one's, and the two versions coexist without a conflict warning (checked).

      Shipping projects only, via $(ShippingProject) in Directory.Build.targets, which is where the
      same test-and-benchmark exclusion already lives for the AOT analyzers.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" Version="5.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="2.0.3" />
    <!--
      3.1.2 is a floor, not a rounding-up. The VSTest adapter declares a catastrophic failure when
      the test process exits and the newest message it has managed to *deliver* is older than a
      crash-detection idle timeout — `CrashDetectionSinkBase.FinishWaitMilliseconds`, hard-coded to
      five seconds in 3.1.1 and raised to sixty (and made configurable) in 3.1.2, in the vendor's
      words "to help eliminate potential false positives related to crashing when running tests on
      slower machines".

      This suite is exactly the shape that trips it. `LiveClientReconnectTests` waits ten seconds of
      wall clock in one test and is the last collection still running, so the Live run's final seven
      seconds carry no messages at all; when the process then exits, the adapter's stdout pump has
      to deliver one message before the main thread's first idle check or the five-second budget is
      already spent. Losing that race on a loaded runner reported `Passed: 217` of 225 and "Test
      process crashed with exit code 0" for a run in which nothing crashed and nothing failed (#62).
      Ten seconds against sixty leaves the margin the five never had.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5" />
    <!--
      Throughput baselines and the allocated-bytes-per-record report for #28. Referenced only by
      benchmarks/, which ships nothing: the *assertion* that the low-level path allocates nothing
      lives in the test projects, where it runs on every `dotnet test`. A benchmark that has to be
      run by hand cannot enforce a guarantee.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="BenchmarkDotNet" Version="0.15.8" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
